## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a scalable governance mechanism spanning policy storage, reconciliation, and enforcement across many namespaces, while preserving auditability and rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Namespace-scoped policy governance
- Central policy store with versioning
- Auto-generation of RBAC Roles, ResourceQuota, and LimitRange
- Real-time enforcement via admission webhook
- Drift detection and safe rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: reconcile NamespaceGuardPolicy and apply RBAC/limits across a namespace
async function reconcile(ns) {
  const policy = fetchPolicy(ns);
  if (!policy) return;
  ensureRBAC(ns, policy.rbac);
  ensureQuota(ns, policy.quota);
  ensureLimits(ns, policy.limits);
  audit(ns, policy.version);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this at scale across thousands of namespaces?
- How would you handle policy propagation latency and eventual consistency?

## Why This Is Asked
Interview context explanation.

## Key Concepts
- CRD design for RegionTrafficPolicy
- Per-namespace traffic steering
- Mesh policy translation (Istio/Linkerd)
- Egress gateways and failover
- Observability: metrics, traces, SLAs
- Drift detection and reconciliation
- Safe rollback via versioned policies
- Testing multi-region failure scenarios

## Code Example
```javascript
// TypeScript sketch
interface RegionTrafficPolicySpec {
  namespaces: string[];
  primaryRegion: string;
  routes: Array<{
    region: string;
    gateway: string;
    latencyBudgetMs?: number;
  }>;
  failoverPolicy?: "conservative"|"aggressive";
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test region outages and gateway misconfigurations?
- How do you resolve policy conflicts when multiple policies apply to a namespace?
- How would you scale the controller in clusters with thousands of namespaces?
